From: Buddy <buddy@workbuddy.ai>
Date: Wed, 30 Jul 2026 14:15:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] risaf: keep clock enabled when encryption is active

The stm32_risaf_init() function unconditionally disables the RISAF
peripheral clock after initialization. When encryption is active
(variant->has_enc = true), the AES encryption/decryption engine
requires the clock to remain running for on-the-fly encrypt/decrypt
of AXI transactions passing through encrypted regions.

In TDCID boot flow on STM32MP2, TF-M BL2 initializes RISAF with
encryption BEFORE DDR initialization. The subsequent DDR data bus
test writes to 0x80000000 (which overlaps with the encrypted tfm_code
region) and reads back the value. With RISAF clock disabled, the
AES engine cannot encrypt/decrypt, causing the readback to return
garbage data and the DDR test to fail with:

  [ERR] [DDR] DDR data bus test: cannot access memory @ 0x80000000

Fix: only disable RISAF clock when encryption is not active.
Encrypted regions need the AES datapath clock continuously because
encryption/decryption is triggered by AXI bus transactions, not by
device driver API calls (the PM framework cannot wake the device
for bus-level accesses).

Signed-off-by: Buddy <buddy@workbuddy.ai>

--- a/platform/ext/target/stm/common/stm32mp2xx/native_driver/src/rif/stm32_risaf.c	2026-07-30 14:22:26.144092773 +0800
+++ b/platform/ext/target/stm/common/stm32mp2xx/native_driver/src/rif/stm32_risaf.c	2026-07-30 14:23:11.953323813 +0800
@@ -814,7 +814,17 @@
 	}
 
 out:
-	clk_disable(clk);
+	/*
+	 * Keep RISAF clock enabled when encryption is configured.
+	 * The AES encryption/decryption datapath needs continuous clock
+	 * because it operates transparently on AXI bus transactions.
+	 * Unlike normal PM-managed devices, RISAF encryption cannot be
+	 * woken up by a device API call -- accesses come through the bus.
+	 * Disabling the clock here would cause encrypted DDR regions to
+	 * become inaccessible (DDR test would fail).
+	 */
+	if (!drv_data->variant->has_enc)
+		clk_disable(clk);
 	if (err)
 		panic();
 
